  "captions": [
    {
      "start": 0.0,
      "end": 3.4500416666666665,
      "text": "How can we add pieces when the pieces have different names?"
    },
    {
      "start": 4.100041666666667,
      "end": 7.311916666666667,
      "text": "Today we are adding and subtracting fractions."
    },
    {
      "start": 7.531916666666667,
      "end": 11.723833333333333,
      "text": "We will use equal-sized wholes and rename parts when we need to."
    },
    {
      "start": 11.943833333333334,
      "end": 15.350666666666667,
      "text": "What should happen to the size of the pieces when we combine them?"
    },
    {
      "start": 16.000666666666667,
      "end": 18.611166666666666,
      "text": "First, use matching denominators."
    },
    {
      "start": 18.831166666666668,
      "end": 22.271708333333333,
      "text": "Two sevenths plus three sevenths makes five sevenths."
    },
    {
      "start": 22.921708333333335,
      "end": 25.982041666666667,
      "text": "We add the numerators and keep the denominator."
    },
    {
      "start": 26.202041666666666,
      "end": 29.366583333333335,
      "text": "We are still counting sevenths, not fourteenths."
    },
    {
      "start": 30.016583333333333,
      "end": 35.3395,
      "text": "For one half plus one quarter, the denominators are related: four is a multiple of two."
    },
    {
      "start": 35.5595,
      "end": 37.759541666666664,
      "text": "Rename the half as two quarters."
    },
    {
      "start": 38.40954166666667,
      "end": 41.15195833333333,
      "text": "Now add two quarters and one quarter."
    },
    {
      "start": 41.37195833333333,
      "end": 43.47408333333333,
      "text": "The result is three quarters."
    },
    {
      "start": 44.12408333333333,
      "end": 46.687875,
      "text": "Subtraction follows the same idea."
    },
    {
      "start": 46.907875,
      "end": 51.316,
      "text": "For three quarters minus one eighth, rename three quarters as six eighths."
    },
    {
      "start": 51.966,
      "end": 53.64041666666667,
      "text": "Take away one eighth."
    },
    {
      "start": 53.860416666666666,
      "end": 55.56720833333333,
      "text": "Five eighths remain."
    },
    {
      "start": 55.78720833333333,
      "end": 57.844375,
      "text": "The denominator stays eight."
    },
    {
      "start": 58.494375,
      "end": 62.42091666666666,
      "text": "On a number line, begin at six eighths and move back one eighth."
    },
    {
      "start": 62.64091666666667,
      "end": 64.64308333333334,
      "text": "You land on five eighths."
    },
    {
      "start": 65.29308333333333,
      "end": 67.09908333333334,
      "text": "Simplify when you can."
    },
    {
      "start": 67.31908333333334,
      "end": 72.12525,
      "text": "One sixth plus three sixths is four sixths, which equals two thirds."
    },
    {
      "start": 72.77525,
      "end": 75.03795833333334,
      "text": "Mixed numbers can make another whole."
    },
    {
      "start": 75.25795833333333,
      "end": 79.294625,
      "text": "One and four fifths plus two fifths is one and six fifths."
    },
    {
      "start": 79.944625,
      "end": 82.407,
      "text": "Regroup five fifths as one whole."
    },
    {
      "start": 82.627,
      "end": 85.25575,
      "text": "The answer is two and one fifth."
    },
    {
      "start": 85.90575,
      "end": 89.56833333333333,
      "text": "For several fractions, rename them with a common denominator."
    },
    {
      "start": 89.78833333333333,
      "end": 95.38291666666667,
      "text": "One half plus one quarter minus one eighth becomes four eighths plus two eighths minus one eighth."
    },
    {
      "start": 96.03291666666667,
      "end": 100.27725,
      "text": "Four plus two minus one is five, so the result is five eighths."
    },
    {
      "start": 100.92725,
      "end": 106.36795833333333,
      "text": "Here is an extension: when neither denominator is a multiple of the other, shared multiples can help."
    },
    {
      "start": 106.58795833333333,
      "end": 109.084,
      "text": "Try one quarter plus one sixth."
    },
    {
      "start": 113.084,
      "end": 116.34941666666667,
      "text": "Multiples of four include four, eight, twelve."
    },
    {
      "start": 116.56941666666667,
      "end": 120.23908333333334,
      "text": "Multiples of six include six, twelve, eighteen."
    },
    {
      "start": 120.45908333333334,
      "end": 123.90616666666666,
      "text": "Twelve is the smallest positive shared multiple."
    },
    {
      "start": 124.55616666666667,
      "end": 126.865375,
      "text": "One quarter is three twelfths."
    },
    {
      "start": 127.085375,
      "end": 129.31175,
      "text": "One sixth is two twelfths."
    },
    {
      "start": 129.53175,
      "end": 131.47933333333333,
      "text": "Add to get five twelfths."
    },
    {
      "start": 132.12933333333334,
      "end": 134.8445,
      "text": "What is two thirds minus one sixth?"
    },
    {
      "start": 138.8445,
      "end": 141.145125,
      "text": "Two thirds is four sixths."
    },
    {
      "start": 141.365125,
      "end": 145.45279166666666,
      "text": "Four sixths minus one sixth is three sixths, or one half."
    },
    {
      "start": 146.45279166666666,
      "end": 150.02725,
      "text": "Use the same-sized whole and same-sized parts."
    },
    {
      "start": 150.24725,
      "end": 155.797,
      "text": "Rename fractions when needed, combine or remove parts, and check the size of the result."
    },
    {
      "start": 156.017,
      "end": 160.93116666666666,
      "text": "Shared-denominator methods beyond related denominators are extension work."
    }
  ],
